<<~ &#x0002; >>

! The Founding Runbook ~ the go-live rite

> A woken operator runs this rite to stand a node fresh-from-void, found the three-kahu
> quorum, arm the charter's pre-rotation, and go live. Each step reads as its own verb; the
> irreversible one wears a warning. The rite runs top to bottom once — a re-run re-founds.

<<~ ahu #the-rite >>

!! The rite ~ six movements

The founding moves through six movements, each closing before the next opens:

<<~loops ✶ clear -> ⏿ found -> ◇ reserve -> ▶ seal-and-back-up -> ↺ regenesis -> LIVE >>

''Clear'' pares the vessel to void · ''found'' stands the device + the three kahu · ''reserve''
forges the pre-rotation's future-key custody · ''seal-and-back-up'' locks the secrets at rest +
writes a recoverable copy · ''regenesis'' re-seeds the content from `bags/` · ''LIVE'' serves.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #clear >>

!! ① Clear ~ pare to void (⚠ irreversible)

A fresh-from-void founding discards the prior identity. ''Back the old identity up FIRST'' — the
vault seals nothing until movement ④, so a raw copy carries the only net before the nuke:

```
lares status                                        # confirm the identity-home path
cp -r ~/.local/state/lares/identity ~/founding-backup-PRIOR   # the only pre-nuke net
lares hooks pause                                   # still the capture/ingest hooks during the pave
```

Then clear. ''⚠ The next line destroys the prior persona-roots + the keyhive archive — it does not
undo.'' A vessel that carries nothing worth keeping (the bootstrap case) crosses it freely:

```
rm -rf ~/.local/state/lares/identity                # ⚠ IRREVERSIBLE: the fresh-from-void cut
lares reset --force                                 # pave the vessel store (the identity home now stands empty)
```

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #found >>

!! ② Found ~ stand the device + the three kahu

`wake --install` stands the device/vessel identity + the daemon + the founder persona-root h0
(h0's root signs the founding bind that peers pin — it MUST pre-exist). The three kahu then stand
as ''three symmetric commands'', each naming its persona so the charter's pet-name join matches all
three (name h0 too — an unnamed founder strands itself out of its own charter):

```
lares wake --install                                # stands the stack + the founder root h0
lares persona new 0 --name 'Guru Joshua Fontany'    # loads + names the pre-standing founder
lares persona new 1 --name 'Telarus, KSC'
lares persona new 2 --name 'The Lindwyrm'
lares persona list                                  # confirm h0/h1/h2 stand, all named
```

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #reserve >>

!! ③ Reserve ~ forge the pre-rotation's future

The charter pre-rotates (a compromised present key-set cannot forge the future,
[[charter-chain|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/charter-chain]]), so `seat` needs a
`--next-key-commit`. `reserve` forges the next epoch's key-set from a ''separate CSPRNG reserve
seed'' (hardened SLIP-0010, never the live signing seed), prints the public commit, splits the seed
Shamir ''2-of-3'', seals Share 1 on the vessel, and zeroizes the seed — the vessel keeps only Share
1 + the public commit, never the seed:

```
lares nexus charter reserve --guardian-a '<name>' --guardian-b '<name>'
    # → prints --next-key-commit + THREE cards (each: a checksummed share code + a confirm phrase),
    #   seals 'Recovery-card mine' (Share 1) on the vessel, zeroizes the seed
```

Place the three cards by hand (#reserve-custody). Then seat the roster + arm the pre-rotation, and
read the quorum verdict:

```
lares nexus charter seat --next-key-commit <the printed commit>   # seats h0/h1/h2 → 2-of-3, epoch0 armed
lares nexus charter show                            # the quorum STANDS + the chain head reads armed
```

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #reserve-custody >>

!! ③′ Reserve custody ~ the independence rule

A single Shamir share reveals ''nothing''; any ''two'' reconstruct the reserve seed. So the whole
scheme rests on one rule:

> ''The three shares MUST sit in three domains that do not fall together.'' No single realistic
> breach, theft, or fire may reach two of them.

The compromise-resistant layout keeps every operator copy as the ''same'' Share 1:

* ''Share 1 (your domain)'' — the vessel + 'Recovery-card mine' (print AND/OR email); all one share,
  one domain. An adversary who owns your whole digital life reads ''one'' share -> nothing.
* ''Share 2'' — 'Recovery-card guardian-A' (a trusted person, or an independent physical place; a
  cabalmate takes it as they launch, via `reserve refresh`).
* ''Share 3'' — 'Recovery-card guardian-B' (a second independent domain).

Two correlation traps hide inside "lots of options": the ''online-identity trap'' (GitHub AND a
cloud both behind one email/password-manager count as ONE domain — put at most one share there) and
the ''co-location trap'' (a thumb drive AND a printout in the same drawer die in one fire — spread
the offline shares across places). The card's ''confirmation phrase'' rides out-of-band (in person, a
call) — it confirms the right person holds the right share, and it becomes the handshake when a
guardian hands the share back at recovery.

<<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 14/20 >> The math then holds three ways: ''compromise'' (you fully
owned -> one share -> safe); ''loss'' (you lose vessel + card -> two guardians rebuild without you);
''rotation'' (your share + one guardian). A recovery materializes the seed, so `reserve refresh`
re-seeds -> re-splits -> re-issues cards + a new commit afterward (Proactive Secret Sharing,
[[siege-resilience|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/siege-resilience]]).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #seal-and-back-up >>

!! ④ Seal + back up ~ lock the secrets, write the net

Set the at-rest passphrase (a no-echo TTY double-entry — a typo self-locks), which seals the keyhive
archive + the recovery/reserve shares. Then write a passphrase-sealed backup to safe media:

```
lares vault seal                                    # no-echo TTY, double-entry; seals the carriers at rest
lares vault export ~/founding-backup.sealed         # a passphrase-SEALED backup (--force overwrites)
lares vault status                                  # confirm the seal state
```

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #regenesis-live >>

!! ⑤ Regenesis + ⑥ LIVE ~ re-seed the content, serve

Regenesis rebirths the CRDT content from `bags/` (identity now stands preserved — the reset lives
inside regenesis, and the identity home rides outside every wipe). Resume the hooks, and the node
serves:

```
lares regenesis --force                             # stop → reset store → re-bake genesis → wake → seed --apply
lares hooks resume                                  # re-arm capture/ingest
lares status                                        # LIVE — the node serves; the quorum + charter stand
```

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-frame >>

!! The frame ~ why compromise-resistance rides early

Recovery cost climbs with the fleet. While a vessel stands as a-multitude-of-one (one laptop-node +
a browser), a compromise costs a ''full-pave + a fresh DID-multitude'' — cheap, nothing accreted. Once
vessel-fleets and cabalmate personaGroups entangle around an operator, the identity carries
relationships no cheap re-mint restores. So the rite ''bakes compromise-resistance in while it costs
nothing to get wrong'', and leans on cheap full-pave as the bootstrap net. The reserve's two guardian
slots stand ''unassigned'' until cabalmates launch, then fill via `reserve refresh` — the seed grows
from a-multitude-of-one, hardening as the cabal arrives.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #identity-classes >>

!! Identity classes ~ who carries the reserve at all

The reserve burden rides ''operator-class'' identities alone (KERI transferable — persistent,
rotatable, they operate node vessels; the charter epoch chain reads as a key-event-log). Browser-only
''users'' (ephemeral, short cap-stacks, no charter role) carry NO reserve — they ride KERI
non-transferable/basic identifiers, and their "rotation" reads as ''abandon-and-recreate'', cheap and
native. <<~ confidence Synthesis 12/20 >> The line draws by persistence/value, not by title: a user
vessel that accretes lasting value drifts toward transferable, held either by promoting that vessel
or by delegated identifiers (the operator root delegates to disposable user streams, durability in
the root). The default holds — users stay light; operators carry the future.

<<~/ahu >>

